eometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/state.json" "file_sha256" "85a68e41755b99591fd81bb18a2bc8610a0a1447bbb6747262ca07e0cc856680"} {"question" "q-165787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=2j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/56a5f95d469cb63e20936a6c07971d9e80611d966282cb37d7ddf17fdc761bab.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $2$ in the $y$-direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/state.json" "file_sha256" "85a68e41755b99591fd81bb18a2bc8610a0a1447bbb6747262ca07e0cc856680"} {"question" "q-165787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b71482df4e5a7ceaa2278712357f7b8769bb9dc5141cdda2dfbfcd1200ebf66d"} {"question" "q-165787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/state.json" "file_sha256" "85a68e41755b99591fd81bb18a2bc8610a0a1447bbb6747262ca07e0cc856680"} {"question" "q-165787" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $2$"] ["te