-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/state.json" "file_sha256" "85a68e41755b99591fd81bb18a2bc8610a0a1447bbb6747262ca07e0cc856680"} {"question" "q-81417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1d25632f24abfc3176f515ba41a763e166d8c9c3101d07732a1efb183a2e9d75.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/52bb468dfeaae8b4c47cb08a7bb08e671e898467e4130e2e243936b599f51704.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6a5da4ecc4dc4cb550649d229f0c7c9d64bd713f901084a937785ea93df86eb1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/9d140b11016d68a8372623e997abdc588633ee02b56a8edee61c8cb269fd4c20.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/a64061e8714d38748ef4907cbdedf873355c64814ef3966d872494e84bcacfbd.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/state.json" "file_sha256" "85a68e41755b99591fd81bb18a2bc8610a0a1447bbb6747262ca07e0cc856680"} {"question" "q-81417" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/9d140b11016d68a8372623e997abdc588633ee02b56a8edee61c8cb269fd4c20.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/state.json" "file_sha256" "85a68e41755b99591fd81bb18a2bc8610a0a1447bbb6747262ca07e0cc856680"} {"question" "e-5450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/0adb71786ea5de6203b155882b20000488d5467cf6dfcd34e6fa4e06ec091e46.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/94fb0fd9708c343c89fd98ff5b87c901c9e795026cc1dbd35d29cff92d6fe59e.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5450.json" "file_sha256" "205c064dfc55b3c6f322fd92b803179f06033168ceee7649eca222fe002c0b19"} {"question" "e-5450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we are told that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ is $4$ times the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{v},$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/779a10caf7c07cbe4b5891240892db5433adc0f9f8895192a3a8bed5f9301126.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5450.json" "file_sha256" "205c064dfc55b3c6f322fd92b803179f06033168ceee7649eca222fe002c0b19"} {"question" "e-5454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5454.json" "file_sha256" "f15d909a5eca3b299f005e0e5b8be3dcefb6e65e213fff2edcd727b1fa6ba301"} {"question" "e-5454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/db15a65fd944c24ea2545baea08e6a0789b749295f623d8d4fdc2f07a37d4b73.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j},$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/1e5310f50dab35bc25447af9b37d972e11eda0afee98ac99b87a819ba62f56a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5454.json" "file_sha256" "f15d909a5eca3b299f005e0e5b8be3dcefb6e65e213fff2edcd727b1fa6ba301"} {"question" "e-5455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/e335abcb649dae3ed9ade01017a23998614c3d0ff5a0898eb9b4d657f68e8b62.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5455.json" "file_sha256" "69d33a11d27ed0b68738b185aa3be41e422c3838bd21922daa757d2e2ba8893d"} {"question" "e-5455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of  $\\mathbf T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5455.json" "file_sha256" "69d33a11d27ed0b68738b185aa3be41e422c3838bd21922daa757d2e2ba8893d"} {"question" "e-5456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/611f958a7bd7475e37f743ef86affde7754195fe2c9ad414e628c17a09d47e38.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/6231eb09a75376d694a6f47c47cf5a26aab4a32c28b2799f7e1f0825a7017f88.png) be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5456.json" "file_sha256" "a81e74604bab8169991811598c113a35bba6d4cce2e20e6077ecd0a50d92841b"} {"question" "e-5456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Looking at the matrix $T,$ we can see that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear of shear factor $-1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/assets/2d3d15c4d6b6f9e08c6fbcc35236b50dcd6cf4d09e9204e0aa255706461c3dd8.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062085/example-5456.json" "file_sha256" "a81e74604bab8169991811598c113a35bba6d4cce2e20e6077ecd0a50d92841b"}] "usage" {"q-38066" [] "q-8092" [] "q-12044" [] "e-5450" [] "q-80726" [] "q-80725" [] "e-5454" [] "q-38052" [] "e-5455" [] "e-5456" []} "basis" 1987 "retractions" [[17592186106418 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, what is the standard matrix of $T$?"] [17592186106419 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, what is the standard matrix of $T$?"] [17592186106235 :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i, j}$ of $R^{2}$. If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i) = i$ and $T(j) = 1.5i + j$, then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T$?"] [17592186105960 :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186106420 :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186106236 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/q-38066.png)\n\nLet $T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$. What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) above under the action of $T$?"] [17592186105539 :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?"] [17592186105539 :question/worked-solution "First, we are told that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$, we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ is $4$ times the $x$-component of $\\mathbf{v}$, while\n- the $y$-components of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a stretch of scale factor $4$ in the $x$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186105540 :question/problem "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\,\\mathbb{R}^2$. If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}$ then, what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$?"] [17592186105540 :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/c28904112781381da0083548d8c2dbbf.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{i})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{j})=\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwe can draw the image of the square under the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/e46ae3a679baae89ff308c327360ee8e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear with a shear factor of $2$."] [17592186105541 :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186105541 :question/worked-solution "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = i \\\\\nT(j) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = 2i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $2$."] [17592186105542 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. What is the image of the square $R$ (shown above) under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/8e226d694c513c348b7136c96c29a52b.png)"] [17592186105542 :question/worked-solution "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a vertical shear of shear factor $-1$. Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1358/Source/Images/6c7707ce3c667c5692b4312d72bc9578.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186102008 17592186102009 17592186102010 17592186102011 17592186102012 17592186103920 17592186103921 17592186103922 17592186103923 17592186103924 17592186103926 17592186103927 17592186103928 17592186103929 17592186103930 17592186105018 17592186105019 17592186105020 17592186105021 17592186105022 17592186105024 17592186105025 17592186105026 17592186105027 17592186105028 17592186105030 17592186105031 17592186105032 17592186105033 17592186105034] "immutable_fields" [[17592186102007 :answer-field/correct] [17592186102007 :answer-field/id] [17592186102007 :answer-field/key] [17592186102007 :answer-field/type] [17592186103919 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103919 :answer-field/id] [17592186103919 :answer-field/key] [17592186103919 :answer-field/type] [17592186103925 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103925 :answer-field/id] [17592186103925 :answer-field/key] [17592186103925 :answer-field/type] [17592186105017 :answer-field/correct] [17592186105017 :answer-field/id] [17592186105017 :answer-field/key] [17592186105017 :answer-field/type] [17592186105023 :answer-field/correct] [17592186105023 :answer-field/id] [17592186105023 :answer-field/key] [17592186105023 :answer-field/type] [17592186105029 :answer-field/correct] [17592186105029 :answer-field/id] [17592186105029 :answer-field/key] [17592186105029 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
